/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/skill-project-management/.task-board/EPIC-260720-1fjsu9_board-workflow-and-spawn-policy-enhancements/STORY-260721-3ecaw4_rate-limit-aware-spawn-agent-selection/TASK-260802-28ydlq_implement-spawn-limits-config-contract/progress.md:118:spawn selection rationale tuple: {"role":"developer","pair":"claude-opus-5","text":"Cycle-3 rework of the spawn limits config contract. Read TASK-260802-28ydlq_review-verdict-cycle-2.md first. CONFIRMED and closed - do not re-open any of it: cycle-1 F1 (the bulk backoff decode turning a failed read into an absence) is genuinely fixed, the per-provider decoder and the UnmarshalJSON route for diagnostic-free callers and the composed entry-point test are all present, and a long list of attacks survived - known-provider ladder written as an object, decreasing ladder, the exact 6h ceiling including the 6h1s boundary, zero and negative and empty ladders, duplicate members modulo whitespace, spread over an empty preferred_agentic_system, every registry-layer refusal with SpawnLimitsGroupTable returning nil, both directions of the layer split, the AC 11 module boundary proven by real import-graph and go.mod parse, the AC 8 production call site driven through resolveSpawnSelectionPreflight, and AC 9 row replacement proven against the composed GroupTable including fall-through to UnmappedGroup. Two NEW blocking findings, both small, both with reviewer probes attached in TASK-260802-28ydlq_reviewer-probes.go.txt. F1 at spawn_limits.go:466-484: the cross-field claim-versus-lease rule lives inside if claimRaw, ok := limits[probe_claim_max_minutes]; ok, so it fires ONLY when the claim key is written. Configure lease 120 and omit the claim entirely and the load is ACCEPTED with effective lease=120 and claim_cap=30 - which is exactly the state the rule exists to forbid. This is not a deliberate scoping choice and you should not defend it as one: the mirror case at line 477 already consults the default and says leaseSource is the default probe_lease_minutes, so the intended semantics are plainly the EFFECTIVE pair and only one half of the class is refused. Report the impact honestly as the reviewer did rather than inflating it - providerlimits/state.go:426-428 raises claimMax to leaseMinutes so the runtime store does not wedge, but THIS task's AC 10 provenance breaks, because ResolveSpawnLimits reports probe_claim_max_minutes=30 origin=default while every Store built from those same values runs 120, and TASK-260802-30qtmx will render a cap the runtime does not use. Either refuse it by comparing the effective cap against the configured lease and naming spawn.limits.probe_lease_minutes in the refusal, or resolve it by mirroring providerlimits and raising the resolved cap with provenance saying the value was DERIVED rather than defaulted; your choice, but whichever you pick needs a test asserting it for the lease-only config above. F2 at spawn_limits.go:738-743 with startup_config.go:163: spawnLimitsDiagnosticIsAdvisory matches on strings.HasPrefix(section, spawn.limits.), and sections are built by joining key paths with dots, so a JSON key that itself contains dots renders into that namespace and inherits the tolerance. Both {\"spawn\":{\"limits.spread\":\"affinity\"}} and {\"spawn.limits.probe_lease_minutes\":15} now load successfully with only a config_section_unknown diagnostic, and BOTH were refused before this change - so this is a fail-closed unknown-key gate that your change turned fail-open for a realistic operator mistake. TestSpawnLimitsUnknownKeyToleranceIsScopedToTheBlock guards only against sibling names and this class walks straight past it. Make the carve-out a structural fact rather than a string one: mark the diagnostic advisory where it is EMITTED inside parseSpawnLimits, for example a field on ConfigDiagnostic or a dedicated code, so a diagnostic that merely renders into the namespace cannot inherit tolerance. Add the dotted-key cases to the scoping test. Two things the reviewer explicitly recorded as NOT findings so you do not chase them: ResolveSpawnLimits having no production caller is in scope because consumption belongs to TASK-260802-17wetr, 178bmr and 30qtmx, and the same for spread_inert; and the clamp at providerlimits/state.go:402-406 lacking the raise its doc comment promises is cosmetic, in another task's file, and mentioned only because F1's impact analysis depends on it. Other agents are editing this checkout - do not revert, stash or clean anything you did not write. Run gates in the FOREGROUND and hand off this turn."}
/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/skill-project-management/.task-board/EPIC-260720-1fjsu9_board-workflow-and-spawn-policy-enhancements/STORY-260721-3ecaw4_rate-limit-aware-spawn-agent-selection/TASK-260802-28ydlq_implement-spawn-limits-config-contract/progress.md:119:spawn selection rationale for claude-opus-5: Cycle-3 rework of the spawn limits config contract. Read TASK-260802-28ydlq_review-verdict-cycle-2.md first. CONFIRMED and closed - do not re-open any of it: cycle-1 F1 (the bulk backoff decode turning a failed read into an absence) is genuinely fixed, the per-provider decoder and the UnmarshalJSON route for diagnostic-free callers and the composed entry-point test are all present, and a long list of attacks survived - known-provider ladder written as an object, decreasing ladder, the exact 6h ceiling including the 6h1s boundary, zero and negative and empty ladders, duplicate members modulo whitespace, spread over an empty preferred_agentic_system, every registry-layer refusal with SpawnLimitsGroupTable returning nil, both directions of the layer split, the AC 11 module boundary proven by real import-graph and go.mod parse, the AC 8 production call site driven through resolveSpawnSelectionPreflight, and AC 9 row replacement proven against the composed GroupTable including fall-through to UnmappedGroup. Two NEW blocking findings, both small, both with reviewer probes attached in TASK-260802-28ydlq_reviewer-probes.go.txt. F1 at spawn_limits.go:466-484: the cross-field claim-versus-lease rule lives inside if claimRaw, ok := limits[probe_claim_max_minutes]; ok, so it fires ONLY when the claim key is written. Configure lease 120 and omit the claim entirely and the load is ACCEPTED with effective lease=120 and claim_cap=30 - which is exactly the state the rule exists to forbid. This is not a deliberate scoping choice and you should not defend it as one: the mirror case at line 477 already consults the default and says leaseSource is the default probe_lease_minutes, so the intended semantics are plainly the EFFECTIVE pair and only one half of the class is refused. Report the impact honestly as the reviewer did rather than inflating it - providerlimits/state.go:426-428 raises claimMax to leaseMinutes so the runtime store does not wedge, but THIS task's AC 10 provenance breaks, because ResolveSpawnLimits reports probe_claim_max_minutes=30 origin=default while every Store built from those same values runs 120, and TASK-260802-30qtmx will render a cap the runtime does not use. Either refuse it by comparing the effective cap against the configured lease and naming spawn.limits.probe_lease_minutes in the refusal, or resolve it by mirroring providerlimits and raising the resolved cap with provenance saying the value was DERIVED rather than defaulted; your choice, but whichever you pick needs a test asserting it for the lease-only config above. F2 at spawn_limits.go:738-743 with startup_config.go:163: spawnLimitsDiagnosticIsAdvisory matches on strings.HasPrefix(section, spawn.limits.), and sections are built by joining key paths with dots, so a JSON key that itself contains dots renders into that namespace and inherits the tolerance. Both {"spawn":{"limits.spread":"affinity"}} and {"spawn.limits.probe_lease_minutes":15} now load successfully with only a config_section_unknown diagnostic, and BOTH were refused before this change - so this is a fail-closed unknown-key gate that your change turned fail-open for a realistic operator mistake. TestSpawnLimitsUnknownKeyToleranceIsScopedToTheBlock guards only against sibling names and this class walks straight past it. Make the carve-out a structural fact rather than a string one: mark the diagnostic advisory where it is EMITTED inside parseSpawnLimits, for example a field on ConfigDiagnostic or a dedicated code, so a diagnostic that merely renders into the namespace cannot inherit tolerance. Add the dotted-key cases to the scoping test. Two things the reviewer explicitly recorded as NOT findings so you do not chase them: ResolveSpawnLimits having no production caller is in scope because consumption belongs to TASK-260802-17wetr, 178bmr and 30qtmx, and the same for spread_inert; and the clamp at providerlimits/state.go:402-406 lacking the raise its doc comment promises is cosmetic, in another task's file, and mentioned only because F1's impact analysis depends on it. Other agents are editing this checkout - do not revert, stash or clean anything you did not write. Run gates in the FOREGROUND and hand off this turn.
/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/skill-project-management/.task-board/EPIC-260720-1fjsu9_board-workflow-and-spawn-policy-enhancements/STORY-260728-2o8wdt_config-feature-flags-section/TASK-260728-13rh9f_setup-never-touches-project-configs-and-fails-loudly/progress.md:92:REMAINING BLOCKER (same harm class, different key): configKeyIsCredentialBearing (startup_config.go:252) omits `auth` and `authorization`. A credential under either key prints verbatim. Reproduced with a NEUTRAL high-entropy value so it is not an artifact of the text pattern matching the probe string: `key remote.auth found string "Zq7Z7pLmN4rW2xTvB9kH"`. This fires under the REAL remote section - the realistic path is an operator writing auth instead of token, exactly the wrong config these refusals exist to catch, so the refusal path is the credential-echo path. Unknown-key diagnostics are blocking (startup_config.go:156), so the repo fails EVERY command until a human edits, printing the credential each time (verified on `tree` and `grep`), and spawned-run stderr is captured into TASK-*_spawn-log_*.log and attached to the board - mechanism unchanged from cycle 1. `Bearer <value>` self-redacts; a bare token does not, and bare is the common shape.
