/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/skill-project-management/.task-board/EPIC-260720-1fjsu9_board-workflow-and-spawn-policy-enhancements/STORY-260728-km72uk_shared-forkable-agent-contexts/TASK-260728-1hdchl_implement-context-publication-validator/progress.md:132:spawn selection rationale tuple: {"role":"developer","pair":"claude-opus-5","text":"Cycle-6 rework, ONE finding left; everything else on this task is confirmed closed, so do not touch it. The provider-enforced budget cap landed and half the contamination requirement did too — what remains is the other half, and it is a narrow gap with a precise cause. The classification rules contain only credential-shaped patterns, so the signed transcript attestation carries credential rule IDs and nothing else, and the contamination scan consumes that attestation as merely credential, inconclusive or clean. Foreign-identity detection meanwhile runs over successful trace request metadata and ledger text. The result is that a transcript can carry another TASK-, STORY-, EPIC-, BUG-, RUN- or CTX- identity while the trace and ledger omit it entirely, and the artifact is admitted clean. Extend the classification rules to derive foreign element, run and context findings over the transcript itself, and make the contamination scan consume those findings rather than collapsing the attestation into three coarse verdicts. Keep the scan identity and digest bound into validation evidence as already implemented. Prove it with the reviewer's own reproduction shape: a foreign identity present ONLY in the transcript, absent from both the trace and the ledger, must quarantine with cold-build-only recovery. Run a mutation study over the new gate as you did on the previous rounds — that practice is what has been closing these findings cleanly."}
/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/skill-project-management/.task-board/EPIC-260720-1fjsu9_board-workflow-and-spawn-policy-enhancements/STORY-260728-km72uk_shared-forkable-agent-contexts/TASK-260728-1hdchl_implement-context-publication-validator/progress.md:133:spawn selection rationale for claude-opus-5: Cycle-6 rework, ONE finding left; everything else on this task is confirmed closed, so do not touch it. The provider-enforced budget cap landed and half the contamination requirement did too — what remains is the other half, and it is a narrow gap with a precise cause. The classification rules contain only credential-shaped patterns, so the signed transcript attestation carries credential rule IDs and nothing else, and the contamination scan consumes that attestation as merely credential, inconclusive or clean. Foreign-identity detection meanwhile runs over successful trace request metadata and ledger text. The result is that a transcript can carry another TASK-, STORY-, EPIC-, BUG-, RUN- or CTX- identity while the trace and ledger omit it entirely, and the artifact is admitted clean. Extend the classification rules to derive foreign element, run and context findings over the transcript itself, and make the contamination scan consume those findings rather than collapsing the attestation into three coarse verdicts. Keep the scan identity and digest bound into validation evidence as already implemented. Prove it with the reviewer's own reproduction shape: a foreign identity present ONLY in the transcript, absent from both the trace and the ledger, must quarantine with cold-build-only recovery. Run a mutation study over the new gate as you did on the previous rounds — that practice is what has been closing these findings cleanly.
/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/skill-project-management/.task-board/EPIC-260720-1fjsu9_board-workflow-and-spawn-policy-enhancements/STORY-260728-km72uk_shared-forkable-agent-contexts/TASK-260728-hdkynb_integrate-context-validation-lifecycle/progress.md:58:spawn selection rationale tuple: {"role":"developer","pair":"claude-opus-5","text":"Rework, four findings, and the first is the one that matters: this task is an INTEGRATION task and nothing is integrated. A repository-wide non-test call search finds only definitions and helper-to-helper calls for the preflight fork checks, the boundary banner and the observer primitives — well-built parts with no production caller. On this story a check that exists but is never reached from a real call path counts as absent, and that has now been found four times; here it applies to the whole delivery. Wire the lifecycle, spawn and observer paths into production and prove each one is reached from the real entry point, not from a test. R2: the seven-day deep audit credits itself with an empty observation — every observed dimension is optional and skipped when empty, yet the audit still advances the clock, increments the count and writes a digest, and a test actually asserts that a deep audit with no observed values restarts the clock. Define the complete deep-audit contract and refuse to credit the deep clock unless every required dimension was genuinely checked; missing or unavailable observations must stay visible rather than becoming a pass; cover omitted-one-dimension and mismatched-one-dimension through the production path, and fix that test rather than deleting it. R3: the Claude observer initializes status to complete and never requires a terminal event, and the size limit does not record truncation, so a stream cut at the limit reaches clean EOF still calling itself complete; the Codex side gets no completion evidence at all. Bind completeness to provider terminal evidence and detect size-limit truncation explicitly, with truncated, unterminated, live-partial, completed and unavailable fixtures for both providers. R4: Write, Edit, NotebookEdit and Codex fileChange are classified as repository READS, so a new file or a blind overwrite inflates read counts, re-exploration episodes and widening recommendations — separate write evidence from read evidence or mark it unclassified and keep it out of read denominators, with create-and-overwrite-without-read fixtures. Remember the asymmetry: this observer must not restrict consumer tools."}
/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/skill-project-management/.task-board/EPIC-260720-1fjsu9_board-workflow-and-spawn-policy-enhancements/STORY-260728-km72uk_shared-forkable-agent-contexts/TASK-260728-hdkynb_integrate-context-validation-lifecycle/progress.md:59:spawn selection rationale for claude-opus-5: Rework, four findings, and the first is the one that matters: this task is an INTEGRATION task and nothing is integrated. A repository-wide non-test call search finds only definitions and helper-to-helper calls for the preflight fork checks, the boundary banner and the observer primitives — well-built parts with no production caller. On this story a check that exists but is never reached from a real call path counts as absent, and that has now been found four times; here it applies to the whole delivery. Wire the lifecycle, spawn and observer paths into production and prove each one is reached from the real entry point, not from a test. R2: the seven-day deep audit credits itself with an empty observation — every observed dimension is optional and skipped when empty, yet the audit still advances the clock, increments the count and writes a digest, and a test actually asserts that a deep audit with no observed values restarts the clock. Define the complete deep-audit contract and refuse to credit the deep clock unless every required dimension was genuinely checked; missing or unavailable observations must stay visible rather than becoming a pass; cover omitted-one-dimension and mismatched-one-dimension through the production path, and fix that test rather than deleting it. R3: the Claude observer initializes status to complete and never requires a terminal event, and the size limit does not record truncation, so a stream cut at the limit reaches clean EOF still calling itself complete; the Codex side gets no completion evidence at all. Bind completeness to provider terminal evidence and detect size-limit truncation explicitly, with truncated, unterminated, live-partial, completed and unavailable fixtures for both providers. R4: Write, Edit, NotebookEdit and Codex fileChange are classified as repository READS, so a new file or a blind overwrite inflates read counts, re-exploration episodes and widening recommendations — separate write evidence from read evidence or mark it unclassified and keep it out of read denominators, with create-and-overwrite-without-read fixtures. Remember the asymmetry: this observer must not restrict consumer tools.
